  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a circular, sunlight-driven system that converts diverse waste and natural feedstocks into useful products. At the top, four inputs are shown—plastics, biomass, water, and chemical waste—feeding into a central reactor or electrochemical cell. The device appears to have two electrodes separated by a membrane, with charge transfer indicated by an electron symbol and arrows. Sunlight shines onto the cell, suggesting that solar energy powers or assists the conversion process.\n\nInside the reactor, colored particles near the electrodes imply chemical reactions occurring on both sides, likely oxidation and reduction steps that transform the incoming materials. The overall message is that low-value or problematic resources can be upgraded through a photo-assisted electrochemical process rather than simply discarded.\n\nThe outputs at the bottom emphasize broad practical use: products for the chemical industry, pharmaceutical compounds, materials or chemicals for indoor applications, and fuels or feedstocks relevant to transportation such as cars, airplanes, and trucks. As a whole, the image highlights a sustainable platform that links waste treatment, renewable energy, and chemical manufacturing, turning multiple kinds of waste into valuable industrial and consumer products.",
